{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.092,
            0.139,
            0.256,
            0.266
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_2_image_0.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.083,
            0.883,
            0.158
          ],
          "content": "These specks of chalk powder can be broken further into smaller particles by further grinding. Let us imagine that this process of grinding continues. Eventually, we would reach a stage where the chalk particles cannot be broken down any further."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.262,
            0.176,
            0.497,
            0.248
          ],
          "content": "Are the units of chalk obtained in this manner considered the smallest u<u>nits of chalk?</u>"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.506,
            0.158,
            0.883,
            0.213
          ],
          "content": "The tiny units obtained at this stage are the basic building blocks that the chalk was made up of."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.507,
            0.215,
            0.883,
            0.27
          ],
          "content": "This means that one whole piece of chalk was made up of a large number of smaller units. These units are called"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.27,
            0.884,
            0.362
          ],
          "content": "constituent particles of chalk. A **constituent particle** is the basic unit that makes up a larger piece of a substance or material. Just like chalk, the grains of sand and clay are not the smallest units of bigger rocks. These are also made up of a large number of their constituent particles."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.256,
            0.365,
            0.488,
            0.384
          ],
          "content": "Let us **explore** further!"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.386,
            0.848,
            0.423
          ],
          "content": "Recall the dissolution of sugar into water to form a solution. What happens to sugar when it is dissolved in water?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.269,
            0.44,
            0.631,
            0.464
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 7.2: Let us perform"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.171,
            0.498,
            0.305,
            0.52
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Safety first"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.067,
            0.53,
            0.169,
            0.61
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_2_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.171,
            0.535,
            0.532,
            0.605
          ],
          "content": "Perform the activity under the supervision of a teacher or an adult. Never eat or drink anything unless asked to."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.559,
            0.484,
            0.882,
            0.522
          ],
          "content": "z Fill a glass tumbler with drinking water."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.559,
            0.527,
            0.882,
            0.565
          ],
          "content": "z Put two teaspoons of sugar into it."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.559,
            0.57,
            0.883,
            0.627
          ],
          "content": "z Do not stir the water. Taste a small spoonful of water from the top layer."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.302,
            0.631,
            0.575,
            0.649
          ],
          "content": "Does the water taste sweet?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.319,
            0.651,
            0.882,
            0.689
          ],
          "content": "z Now, stir the water until the sugar dissolves completely (Fig. 7.2)."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.066,
            0.693,
            0.291,
            0.854
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.112,
                0.858,
                0.267,
                0.891
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 7.2: Dissolving sugar in water"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_2_image_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.317,
            0.694,
            0.835,
            0.714
          ],
          "content": "z Again taste a spoonful of water from the top layer."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.301,
            0.718,
            0.879,
            0.737
          ],
          "content": "What difference in taste do you notice? Does it taste sweet?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.301,
            0.739,
            0.883,
            0.795
          ],
          "content": "Since the top layer of water tastes sweet after dissolving sugar, it must be present in the solution. Do you observe any sugar particles in the solution?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.3,
            0.797,
            0.884,
            0.891
          ],
          "content": "Sugar particles can no longer be observed but their presence can be sensed by taste. When sugar dissolves in water, it breaks up into its constituent particles which cannot be broken down further. Each tiny grain of sugar is made up of millions and millions of such constituent particles."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.065,
            0.924,
            0.099,
            0.938
          ],
          "content": "100"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.001,
            0.94,
            0.16,
            0.999
          ],
          "content": ""
        },
        {
